<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Built with WiX Toolset v3 (candle.exe -ext WixUtilExtension -ext
  WixUIExtension, then light.exe -ext WixUtilExtension -ext WixUIExtension
  -sice:ICE20), preinstalled on the windows-latest GitHub Actions runner
  image, so no third-party build tool needs to be installed in CI.
  WixUIExtension is linked for the stock ExitDialog/UserExit/FatalError
  dialogs and the WixUI_ErrorProgressText fragment below, not for a full
  wizard.

  -sice:ICE20 is still required, even with those stock dialogs present:
  ICE20 additionally demands a FilesInUse dialog and entries in
  AdminUISequence (a network/administrative install this MSI has never
  supported), neither of which exist here. Building those by hand only to
  satisfy a build-time lint, rather than suppressing the check, would mean
  adopting parts of a full WixUI wizard for an MSI that otherwise shows no
  wizard flow at all — suppressing ICE20 does not change runtime behaviour,
  it only skips light.exe's own validation of a feature (admin installs)
  this package deliberately does not offer.

  Suppressing ICE20 was first thought not to regress anything: reasoned,
  not verified, and wrong in one respect. On real hardware (0.2.15 -> 0.2.16
  upgrade, 2026-08-19), the missing FilesInUse dialog is not only a build-time
  check. Restart Manager detects the running service's locked binary before
  StopServiceCA gets to stop it, tries to show its own "files in use" prompt,
  finds no authored dialog and no ErrorDialog property to fall back to, and
  the engine's actual fallback is a visible message box: "The installer has
  encountered an unexpected error installing this package... error code is
  2803", confusing though not fatal; the install still completes. Fixed by
  disabling Restart Manager for this package (MSIRESTARTMANAGERCONTROL below)
  rather than authoring the dialogs ICE20 wants: StopServiceCA already stops
  the service deterministically before RemoveFiles, so Restart Manager's own
  detection is redundant here, and removing the trigger is smaller than
  building a FilesInUse dialog just to satisfy it.

  Variables supplied at candle.exe time (-d<Name>=<Value>):
    Version              MSI ProductVersion, numeric major.minor.build only
    BinaryPath            path to the built smtprelayd.exe (windows/amd64)
    ExampleConfigPath      path to configs/smtprelayd.example.toml

  Registers the Windows service by running "smtprelayd.exe install" /
  "uninstall" as deferred custom actions, rather than WiX's own
  ServiceInstall element: the SCM registration must match exactly what
  cmd/smtprelayd/service_windows.go configures (service name, the
  NT SERVICE\smtprelayd virtual account, recovery action), and keeping that
  in one place avoids the two drifting apart. The service is installed but
  never started automatically: it has no usable configuration until the
  operator edits smtprelayd.toml.

  The data directory ACL is set the same way, by "smtprelayd.exe
  secure-datadir", and for the same reason: config.CheckDataDirACL refuses to
  start the service unless the DACL matches, so the side that writes it and
  the side that verifies it are one piece of code. util:PermissionEx was used
  here before and left the directory inheriting from %ProgramData%, which
  carries BUILTIN\Users:(OI)(CI)(RX); it adds ACEs but does not set
  PROTECTED_DACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION, so no install ever passed the check.

  Uninstall does not remove %ProgramData%\SMTPRelayd by default: the spool
  may still hold accepted, undelivered mail, and the history database is the
  only record of it. PurgeDataDlg asks the operator during an interactive
  uninstall whether to delete it anyway; "yes" sets CLEANDATA=1, which gates
  PurgeDataDirCA ("smtprelayd.exe purge-datadir") in InstallExecuteSequence.
  The dialog only ever shows for a real, interactive, top-level uninstall
  (REMOVE="ALL" AND NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE, and InstallUISequence does not
  run at all under msiexec /qn or in an upgrade's nested removal), so a
  silent or scripted uninstall never deletes data unless CLEANDATA=1 is
  passed explicitly on the command line.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/wix/2006/wi">

  <Product Id="*"
           Name="smtprelayd"
           Language="1033"
           Version="$(var.Version)"
           Manufacturer="Tokajer"
           UpgradeCode="64270ec1-058e-489e-bd2d-284e42cb37af">

    <Package InstallerVersion="500" Compressed="yes" InstallScope="perMachine" Platform="x64" />

    <MajorUpgrade
      Schedule="afterInstallInitialize"
      DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of smtprelayd is already installed." />

    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" />

    <!-- Answer to PurgeDataDlg. Unset (falsy) unless the operator clicks
         "Yes, delete it" or passes it explicitly on the command line for a
         scripted uninstall, e.g. msiexec /x smtprelayd.msi /qn CLEANDATA=1. -->
    <Property Id="CLEANDATA" Value="0" />

    <!-- Restart Manager's own "files in use" detection is redundant here:
         StopServiceCA below already stops the service deterministically
         before RemoveFiles runs, which is what actually unlocks the binary
         for an upgrade. On real hardware it surfaced instead as a visible
         "installer has encountered an unexpected error... error code is
         2803" message box, because this package has no authored FilesInUse
         dialog for it to show instead (see the -sice:ICE20 note above).
         Disabling Restart Manager removes the trigger for that fallback
         rather than building a dialog just to satisfy it. -->
    <Property Id="MSIRESTARTMANAGERCONTROL" Value="Disable" />

    <Directory Id="TARGETDIR" Name="SourceDir">
      <Directory Id="ProgramFiles64Folder">
        <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="SMTPRelayd" />
      </Directory>
      <Directory Id="CommonAppDataFolder">
        <Directory Id="DATAFOLDER" Name="SMTPRelayd" />
      </Directory>
    </Directory>

    <DirectoryRef Id="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <Component Id="ServiceBinary" Guid="*" Win64="yes">
        <File Id="smtprelaydEXE" Source="$(var.BinaryPath)" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </DirectoryRef>

    <DirectoryRef Id="DATAFOLDER">
      <Component Id="ExampleConfig" Guid="*" Win64="yes">
        <File Id="ExampleConfigFile" Name="smtprelayd.toml.example"
              Source="$(var.ExampleConfigPath)" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>

      <!-- Explicit GUID, not "*": the KeyPath of this component is the
           directory itself, for which WiX cannot derive a stable GUID.
           Never regenerate it, or an upgrade will create a second folder. -->
      <!-- No RemoveFolder: the directory holds the spool, and a spool can
           still hold undelivered mail plus the history database at uninstall
           time. Removing it would discard mail the relay accepted and
           acknowledged. The operator deletes it deliberately or not at all. -->
      <Component Id="DataFolder" Guid="31B78A8A-3A73-417F-A500-5E8FEBFD9416" Win64="yes">
        <CreateFolder />
      </Component>
    </DirectoryRef>

    <Feature Id="Complete" Title="smtprelayd" Level="1">
      <ComponentRef Id="ServiceBinary" />
      <ComponentRef Id="ExampleConfig" />
      <ComponentRef Id="DataFolder" />
    </Feature>

    <CustomAction Id="InstallServiceCA" FileKey="smtprelaydEXE" ExeCommand="install"
                  Execute="deferred" Impersonate="no" Return="check" />
    <CustomAction Id="StopServiceCA" FileKey="smtprelaydEXE" ExeCommand="stop"
                  Execute="deferred" Impersonate="no" Return="ignore" />
    <CustomAction Id="UninstallServiceCA" FileKey="smtprelaydEXE" ExeCommand="uninstall"
                  Execute="deferred" Impersonate="no" Return="ignore" />
    <CustomAction Id="SecureDataDirCA" FileKey="smtprelaydEXE" ExeCommand="secure-datadir"
                  Execute="deferred" Impersonate="no" Return="check" />
    <CustomAction Id="PurgeDataDirCA" FileKey="smtprelaydEXE" ExeCommand="purge-datadir"
                  Execute="deferred" Impersonate="no" Return="ignore" />

    <!-- Deliberately not built on a full WixUI wizard (WixUI_InstallDir or
         similar): the rest of this MSI shows no Welcome/License/feature-tree
         pages, and pulling one in for one yes/no question would be a much
         bigger change than asked for. WixUI_ErrorProgressText is pulled in
         for its ActionText/Error table entries (progress messages, standard
         error text) and the stock ExitDialog/UserExit/FatalError dialogs
         below are the same shared dialogs any WixUI wizard uses, referenced
         from WixUIExtension rather than hand-authored: without them, both
         install and uninstall reached the end of ExecuteAction and simply
         closed with no confirmation at all, which read as "did this actually
         finish?" Their Show entries reuse the exact same
         InstallUISequence table PurgeDataDlg already sequences into. -->
    <UI>
      <TextStyle Id="DefaultFont" FaceName="Tahoma" Size="8" />
      <Property Id="DefaultUIFont" Value="DefaultFont" />
      <!-- WixUI_Common supplies the shared Binary (dialog background bitmap)
           and TextStyle (WixUI_Font_Bigger etc.) entries ExitDialog/
           FatalError/UserExit reference internally; WixUI_ErrorProgressText
           is the separate fragment for the Error/ActionText tables. Neither
           pulls the other in, both are needed. -->
      <UIRef Id="WixUI_Common" />
      <UIRef Id="WixUI_ErrorProgressText" />
      <!-- ExitDialog's own Finish button ships with no ControlEvent wired
           (WiX leaves that to whichever top-level wizard consumes it, since
           different wizards want different finish behaviour); we only need
           it to close the dialog. -->
      <Publish Dialog="ExitDialog" Control="Finish" Event="EndDialog" Value="Return">1</Publish>
      <Dialog Id="PurgeDataDlg" Width="270" Height="105" Title="smtprelayd Setup" NoMinimize="yes">
        <Control Id="Question" Type="Text" X="20" Y="15" Width="230" Height="45" NoPrefix="yes"
                 Text="Also delete the data directory (%ProgramData%\SMTPRelayd: spool and message history)? This cannot be undone." />
        <Control Id="YesButton" Type="PushButton" X="90" Y="70" Width="80" Height="17" Text="Yes, delete it">
          <Publish Property="CLEANDATA" Value="1">1</Publish>
          <Publish Event="EndDialog" Value="Return">1</Publish>
        </Control>
        <Control Id="NoButton" Type="PushButton" X="180" Y="70" Width="70" Height="17" Text="No" Default="yes" Cancel="yes">
          <Publish Property="CLEANDATA" Value="0">1</Publish>
          <Publish Event="EndDialog" Value="Return">1</Publish>
        </Control>
      </Dialog>
    </UI>

    <InstallUISequence>
      <!-- Same condition UninstallServiceCA runs under below: a genuine
           top-level uninstall, never an upgrade's nested removal of the old
           product. InstallUISequence itself does not run under msiexec /qn,
           which is what keeps a silent uninstall from ever showing this. -->
      <Show Dialog="PurgeDataDlg" Before="ExecuteAction">REMOVE="ALL" AND NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE</Show>
      <!-- Stock WixUIExtension dialogs, not hand-authored: give both install
           and uninstall a closing screen instead of the wizard silently
           disappearing once ExecuteAction finishes. OnExit applies to
           whichever action actually ran (INSTALL or the REMOVE=ALL path),
           since InstallUISequence is shared between both. -->
      <Show Dialog="ExitDialog" OnExit="success" />
      <Show Dialog="UserExit" OnExit="cancel" />
      <Show Dialog="FatalError" OnExit="error" />
    </InstallUISequence>

    <InstallExecuteSequence>
      <!-- Only on a genuinely fresh install: never on repair (Installed is
           already set) and never when upgrading an earlier version, so an
           in-place version bump does not re-register a service the SCM
           already holds. UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE is not usable here: the engine
           only sets it in the nested RemoveExistingProducts call that
           removes the *old* product, never in this (the new product's) own
           execute sequence, so "NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE" was always true
           here and this action ran on every upgrade too, which is exactly
           what broke the 2026-08-18 upgrade, since the old registration is
           deliberately left in place by UninstallServiceCA below and
           "install" over an existing registration fails.
           WIX_UPGRADE_DETECTED is the property MajorUpgrade's FindRelatedProducts
           sets (and propagates as a secure property) in this product's own
           sequence when an earlier version is present. -->
      <Custom Action="InstallServiceCA" After="InstallFiles">NOT Installed AND NOT WIX_UPGRADE_DETECTED</Custom>
      <!-- After InstallServiceCA, because the ACE for the NT SERVICE\smtprelayd
           virtual account can only be resolved once the service exists. Runs on
           repair and upgrade as well: an ACL widened by hand between versions
           is exactly the case that must be repaired. -->
      <Custom Action="SecureDataDirCA" After="InstallServiceCA">NOT REMOVE="ALL"</Custom>
      <!-- Runs on a plain uninstall AND on the old side of a major upgrade:
           the running service locks smtprelayd.exe, so it must be stopped
           before RemoveFiles or the subsequent InstallFiles of the new
           version fails to replace the binary. Only the registration
           removal below is skipped during an upgrade. -->
      <Custom Action="StopServiceCA" Before="RemoveFiles">REMOVE="ALL"</Custom>
      <Custom Action="UninstallServiceCA" After="StopServiceCA">REMOVE="ALL" AND NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE</Custom>
      <!-- CLEANDATA is only ever "1" if the operator clicked "Yes, delete
           it" on PurgeDataDlg, or passed it explicitly for a scripted
           uninstall; it is never set during an upgrade's nested removal,
           which never runs InstallUISequence at all. -->
      <Custom Action="PurgeDataDirCA" After="UninstallServiceCA">REMOVE="ALL" AND NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE AND CLEANDATA="1"</Custom>
    </InstallExecuteSequence>

  </Product>
</Wix>
